 reachability, and what failure visibility is lost by aggregation. Make monitoring aware of that distinction.</p><h4>Cross-vendor lesson</h4><p>Cisco and Arista fabrics face the same scale tradeoff even when the exact policy knob differs. Route granularity is an architecture decision, not merely a BGP optimization.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show route table bgp.evpn.0
show route &lt;subnet&gt;
show evpn database</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 18, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">FRRouting</span><h3>19. EVPN depends on Linux bridge/VRF state</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/evpn.html">https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/evpn.html</a></p><h4>FRR architecture</h4><p>FRRouting provides the EVPN control plane but commonly relies on Linux kernel objects for the data plane. L2VNIs map to MAC-VRF/bridge/VXLAN constructs; L3VNIs map to IP-VRF relationships. Correct BGP EVPN state therefore depends on correct Linux bridge, SVI, VRF, FDB, neighbor, and route state.</p><h4>Why this is different operationally</h4><p>On an integrated switch NOS, many of those objects are managed behind one CLI. With FRR, it is common to inspect both FRR and native Linux commands. A route can exist in `show bgp l2vpn evpn` while the kernel bridge or VRF association is wrong.</p><h4>Scale caveat</h4><p>FRR documentation calls out Linux neighbor-cache considerations for larger EVPN host counts. A control plane can scale logically while the host OS begins aggressive garbage collection or neighbor-table exhaustion.</p><h4>Troubleshooting</h4><p>Inspect EVPN routes, Linux links, bridge FDB, neighbor table, VRF routes, and VXLAN devices together. The first inconsistent object is usually more valuable than another BGP reset.</p><h4>Interoperability</h4><p>Remote hardware VTEPs see normal EVPN/VXLAN behavior and cannot diagnose the Linux-local failure. That is why multivendor incident response must include platform-local data-plane evidence.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show bgp l2vpn evpn
ip link
bridge fdb show
ip neigh
ip route show vrf &lt;vrf&gt;</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 19, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">FRRouting</span><h3>20. OSPF redistribution by area type</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/ospfd.html">https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/ospfd.html</a></p><h4>Redistribution into OSPF</h4><p>FRR OSPF supports redistribution from several routing sources and can apply route maps, metric values, and E1/E2 metric type. The resulting external route representation depends on OSPF area type as well as redistribution policy.</p><h4>Area-type behavior</h4><p>Normal external-capable areas carry Type-5 LSAs. NSSAs use Type-7 LSAs for locally introduced externals, which can be translated at the NSSA boundary. Stub areas intentionally do not receive ordinary external LSAs. An absent route in a stub area may therefore be correct design behavior.</p><h4>Troubleshooting</h4><p>Prove the source route, route-map match, generated external LSA type, ABR behavior, and receiving-area policy. Do not treat every missing external as a redistribution failure.</p><h4>Metric semantics</h4><p>Choose E1/E2 intentionally. E1 adds internal cost toward the ASBR, while E2 typically keeps the external metric dominant; the choice affects path selection in multi-exit designs.</p><h4>Cross-vendor lesson</h4><p>These are protocol-standard OSPF concepts and should be recognizable across Cisco, Juniper, Arista, Aruba, and Fortinet even though command syntax differs.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show ip route
show route-map
show ip ospf database external
show ip ospf database nssa-external</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 20, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><article class="lesson"><span class="badge">FRRouting</span><h3>21. IS-IS Segment Routing capability matrix</h3><p><b>Source:</b> <a href="https://docs.frrouting.org/en/stable-10.0/isisd.html">https://docs.frrouting.org/en/stable-10.0/isisd.html</a></p><h4>FRR Segment Routing model</h4><p>FRR documents IS-IS Segment Routing for the MPLS data plane, including Prefix-SID advertisement, SRGB configuration, and node MSD. This allows FRR to participate in standards-based SR labs with other routers.</p><h4>Capability matrix matters</h4><p>The documented version also lists limitations such as missing optional capabilities or restricted algorithms/label blocks. A device saying “Segment Routing supported” does not imply parity with every feature available on Cisco or Juniper.</p><h4>Control/data-plane sequence</h4><p>IS-IS advertises SID information in TLVs, the local routing process selects paths, the system allocates/programs MPLS labels, and packets follow the label stack. Troubleshooting must therefore prove both IS-IS advertisement and MPLS forwarding state.</p><h4>Interoperability testing</h4><p>Compare Prefix-SID advertisement, SRGB assumptions, label values, node MSD, and supported algorithms across FRR and peer vendors. Test failure and reconvergence rather than validating only a steady-state ping.</p><h4>Design guidance</h4><p>Build a feature matrix before production: adjacency, SR-MPLS data plane, Prefix-SID, SRGB/SRLB, TI-LFA or algorithm support, binding SID, SRMS, and level behavior. Standards compliance is the starting point; optional feature parity determines deployability.</p><details open=""><summary>Verification commands / evidence</summary><pre>show isis database detail
show mpls table
segment-routing on</pre></details><p><b>Reproducible lab: </b>Build the smallest reproducible lab for lesson 21, establish a known-good baseline, break exactly one dependency described above, capture the first changed control-plane and data-plane evidence, remediate it, and verify recovery without changing unrelated features.</p><div class="remember">Active recall: identify the first failing state boundary, the exact evidence that proves it, and the smallest correction that restores the intended design.</div></article><h2>Original source figures</h2><div class="grid"><div class="fig"><span class="badge">Cisco</span><h3>EVPN fabric topology</h3><a href="https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/catalyst_standalones/Multihoming/multihoming-in-bgp-evpn-vxlan-fabric/routed-overlay-mh/routed-overlay-network-reference-configuration.html"><img alt="EVPN fabric topology" onerror="badimg(this)" src="https://www.cisco.com/c/dam/en/us/td/i/300001-400000/350001-360000/359001-360000/359667.jpg"/></a><div class="fb"><a href="https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/catalyst_standalones/Multihoming/multihoming-in-bgp-evpn-vxlan-fabric/routed-overlay-mh/routed-overlay-network-reference-configuration.html">Open original source figure</a></div><p><b>What to notice:</b> Separate access, underlay and tenant overlay state.</p><div class="source-links"><p><b>Source article: </b><a href="https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/catalyst_standalones/Multihoming/multihoming-in-bgp-evpn-vxlan-fabric/routed-overlay-mh/routed-overlay-network-reference-configuration.html">https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/catalyst_standalones/Multihoming/multihoming-in-bgp-evpn-vxlan-fabric/routed-overlay-mh/routed-overlay-network-reference-configuration.html</a></p><p><b>Original image URL: </b><a href="https://www.cisco.com/c/dam/en/us/td/i/300001-400000/350001-360000/359001-360000/359667.jpg">https://www.cisco.com/c/dam/en/us/td/i/300001-400000/350001-360000/359001-360000/359667.jpg</a></p></div></div><div class="fig"><span class="badge">Fortinet</span><h3>BGP + SD-WAN dual-path topology</h3><a href="https://community.fortinet.com/fortigate-3/technical-tip-configuring-sd-wan-traffic-steering-with-bgp-learned-routes-229549"><img alt="BGP + SD-WAN dual-path topology" onerror="badimg(this)" src="https://fortinet.grazitti.com/fortinet-kb-production/api/uploads/f463303c.png"/></a><div class="fb"><a href="https://community.fortinet.com/fortigate-3/technical-tip-configuring-sd-wan-traffic-steering-with-bgp-learned-routes-229549">Open original source figure</a></div><p><b>What to notice:</b> Both BGP peers can be up before both next hops are installed.</p><div class="source-links"><p><b>Source article: </b><a href="https://community.fortinet.com/fortigate-3/technical-tip-configuring-sd-wan-traffic-steering-with-bgp-learned-routes-229549">https://community.fortinet.com/fortigate-3/technical-tip-configuring-sd-wan-traffic-steering-with-bgp-learned-routes-229549</a></p><p><b>Original image URL: </b><a href="https://fortinet.grazitti.com/fortinet-kb-production/api/uploads/f463303c.png">https://fortinet.grazitti.com/fortinet-kb-production/api/uploads/f463303c.png</a></p></div></div><div class="fig"><span class="badge">Palo Alto Networks</span><h3>No-Redist policy example</h3><a href="https://live.paloaltonetworks.com/t5/general-topics/how-to-filter-routes-being-exported-to-bgp-neighbor/td-p/578873"><img alt="No-Redist policy example" onerror="badimg(this)" src="https://live.paloaltonetworks.com/t5/image/serverpage/image-id/57951i89F9808DD1BFC786/image-size/medium/is-moderation-mode/true?px=900&amp;v=v2"/></a><div class="fb"><a href="https://live.paloaltonetworks.com/t5/general-topics/how-to-filter-routes-being-exported-to-bgp-neighbor/td-p/578873">Open original source figure</a></div><p><b>What to notice:</b> Global redistribution exclusion is different from peer export policy.</p><div class="source-links"><p><b>Source article: </b><a href="https://live.paloaltonetworks.com/t5/general-topics/how-to-filter-routes-being-exported-to-bgp-neighbor/td-p/578873">https://live.paloaltonetworks.com/t5/general-topics/how-to-filter-routes-being-exported-to-bgp-neighbor/td-p/578873</a></p><p><b>Original image URL: </b><a href="https://live.paloaltonetworks.com/t5/image/serverpage/image-id/57951i89F9808DD1BFC786/image-size/medium/is-moderation-mode/true?px=900&amp;v=v2">https://live.paloaltonetworks.com/t5/image/serverpage/image-id/57951i89F9808DD1BFC786/image-size/medium/is-moderation-mode/true?px=900&amp;v=v2</a></p></div></div><div class="fig"><span class="badge">Arista Networks</span><h3>EVPN VXLAN IRB sample</h3><a href="https://www.arista.com/en/um-eos/eos-sample-configurations"><img alt="EVPN VXLAN IRB sample" onerror="badimg(this)" src="https://www.arista.com/assets/data/images/EVPN%20L3%20Basic%20Diagram.jpg"/></a><div class="fb"><a href="https://www.arista.com/en/um-eos/eos-sample-configurations">Open original source figu